=== Energy ===
Asia's electricity demand has quadrupled since 2000 (rising from 4,199 TWh to over 16,000 TWh in 2024). This growth, driven largely by China and increasingly by India, Indonesia, and Viet Nam,<ref name=":02">{{Cite web |title=Global Electricity Review 2025|url=https://ember-energy.org/latest-insights/global-electricity-review-2025|access-date=2026-03-18|website=Ember|language=en-US}}</ref> makes Asia the fastest-growing region for electricity demand globally, at about 5% per year.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Asia|url=https://ember-energy.org/countries-and-regions/asia|access-date=2026-03-18|website=Ember|language=en-US}}</ref> The region now accounts for about 36% of global [[World energy supply and consumption|primary energy consumption]], a share projected to approach 50% by 2050.<ref name="AR6_WGII_Chapter10">{{cite book |author1=[[Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change]]|title=Climate Change 2022 – Impacts, Adaptation and Vulnerability|date=2023|isbn=978-1-009-32584-4|pages=1457–1580|chapter=Asia|doi=10.1017/9781009325844.012|chapter-url=https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg2/downloads/report/IPCC_AR6_WGII_Chapter10.pdf|doi-access=free}}</ref><sup>: 1468</sup> [[Fossil fuel]]s remain central in Asia: 66% of its energy is generated from fossil fuels, and Asia makes up 83% of the world's coal generation.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |title=Asia|url=https://ember-energy.org/countries-and-regions/asia|access-date=2026-03-18|website=Ember|language=en-US|date=13 June 2025}}</ref> However, coal is slowly being phased out and [[natural gas]] is displacing oil and oil products in various sectors, supported by policies and regulations. Natural gas demand in emerging market and developing economies in Asia expanded by around 6% in 2024, accounting for nearly 40% of incremental global gas demand.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |last=IEA|title=Natural gas: Global Energy Review 2025|url=https://www.iea.org/reports/global-energy-review-2025/natural-gas|access-date=2026-03-31|website=IEA|language=en-GB}}</ref> By 2040, Asia is expected to account for the majority of global coal use and over a quarter of natural gas consumption.<ref name="AR6_WGII_Chapter10" /><sup>: 1468</sup>

At the same time, Asia is the global leader in [[renewable energy]] expansion. It is currently the only region on track to meet the goal of tripling renewable capacity by 2030, largely due to China, the leading nation in renewable energy generation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Climate Analytics|date=13 February 2024|title=Tripling renewables by 2030: interpreting the global goal at the regional level|url=https://climateanalytics.org/publications/tripling-renewables-by-2030-interpreting-the-global-goal-at-the-regional-level|access-date=2026-04-02|website=Climate Analytics|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=IEA|title=COP28 Tripling Renewable Capacity Pledge|url=https://www.iea.org/reports/cop28-tripling-renewable-capacity-pledge/executive-summary|access-date=2026-04-02|website=IEA|language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Yearly Electricity Data |url=https://ember-energy.org/data/yearly-electricity-data |access-date=2026-04-02 |website=Ember |language=en-US}}</ref> China's wind and solar capacity has surged in recent years, together overtaking that of coal,<ref name=":4">{{Cite book |last=Ember|url=https://ember-energy.org/app/uploads/2025/09/China-Energy-Transition-Review-2025.pdf|title=China Anergy Transition 2025|date=9 September 2025|publisher=Ember}}</ref> while in 2024 India become the world's third largest generator of electricity from wind and solar.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Ember|title=India|url=https://ember-energy.org/countries-and-regions/india|access-date=2026-03-31|website=Ember|language=en-US}}</ref> Nearly half of the world's new renewable energy capacity is built in Asia.<ref name="AR6_WGII_Chapter10" /><sup>: 1470 </sup>

=== Tourism ===
[[File:วัดพระศรีรัตนศาสดาราม-5.jpg|thumb|alt=A Thai temple complex with several ornate buildings, and a lot of visitors|[[Wat Phra Kaew]] in the [[Grand Palace]] is among [[Bangkok]]'s major tourist attractions.]]
