'''Agnes of Merania''' (1175 – July 1201) was [[Queen of France]] by marriage to King [[Philip II of France|Philip II]].

{{Short description|Queen of France from 1196 to 1201}}

She is called Marie by some of the French chroniclers.{{sfn|McAuliffe|2012|p=197}}

==Biography==
Agnes Maria was the daughter of [[Berthold, Duke of Merania]]{{sfn|Powell|2004|p=66}} and [[Agnes of Rochlitz]].{{sfn|Peters|1971|p=52}}

In June 1196, Agnes married [[Philip II of France]], who had repudiated his second wife [[Ingeborg of Denmark, Queen of France|Ingeborg of Denmark]] in 1193.{{sfn|Bradbury|1997|p=183}} [[Pope Innocent III]] espoused the cause of Ingeborg; but Philip did not submit until 1200, when, nine months after [[Interdict (Catholic canon law)|interdict]] had been added to [[Excommunication (Catholic Church)|excommunication]], he consented to a separation from Agnes.{{sfn|McDougall|2017|p=223}}

Agnes died, possibly in childbirth, in July of the next year, at the castle of [[Poissy]], and was buried in the Convent of St. Corentin, near [[Mantes-la-Jolie|Mantes]].{{sfn|McDougall|2017|p=223}}

==Family==
Agnes and Philip had two children: 
*[[Marie of France, Duchess of Brabant|Mary]], b. 1198{{sfn|McDougall|2017|p=223}}
*[[Philip I, Count of Boulogne]], b 1200{{sfn|McDougall|2017|p=223}}

Both were legitimized by the Pope in 1201.{{sfn|Hallam|1980|p=196}} 



